      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-ayodhya-kanda-0326",
      "sequence": 703,
      "meaning": "His body thrilled, Sītā and Raghubīra dwelt in his heart, the Name was upon his tongue, and tears filled his eyes. While Lakṣmaṇa, Rāma, and Sītā lived in the forest, Bharata dwelt at home and disciplined his body through austerity. Considering both sides, everyone said that Bharata was worthy of praise in every way. Hearing of his vows and observances made saints feel abashed; seeing his condition put sage-kings to shame. Bharata's supremely pure conduct is sweet, lovely, joyful, and productive of blessing. It removes the difficult stain and anguish of the Kali age and is a sun that destroys the night of great delusion. It is a lion before the elephant of accumulated sin, the pacifier of every host of afflictions, a delight to the Lord's servants, a breaker of the burden of worldly existence, and the very essence of the nectar-moon of love for Rāma.",
